1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,572
Here's the voltage induced in one phase as
a magnet rotor turns.

2
00:00:04,612 --> 00:00:09,710
Two windings, same rotor, same number of
turns: only their slots differ.

3
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:12,312
With each pole's turns in one pair of
slots,

4
00:00:12,352 --> 00:00:15,550
the voltage copies the flat-topped magnet
field.

5
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:19,227
Spread them over neighbouring slots and
shorten the coils,

6
00:00:19,267 --> 00:00:24,888
and the flat top disappears: closer to a
sine, with a fundamental about 7% smaller.

7
00:00:24,928 --> 00:00:29,969
Today we'll predict both effects with one
number per harmonic: the winding factor.

8
00:00:30,969 --> 00:00:33,452
First, the answers from Lesson 5.

9
00:00:33,492 --> 00:00:36,827
One: wider slot openings raise Carter's
coefficient,

10
00:00:36,867 --> 00:00:39,694
so the average gap flux density falls.

11
00:00:39,734 --> 00:00:45,636
Two: a flat-topped wave of height B,
spanning 160 electrical degrees,

12
00:00:45,676 --> 00:00:51,977
has a fundamental of 4 over π, times sine
80°, times B: about 1.25 B.

13
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:56,378
Three: at 1500 rpm the rotor turns 25
times a second,

14
00:00:56,418 --> 00:01:01,569
each turn bringing 4 pole pairs past a
tooth: 100 hertz.

15
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:07,447
Start with one conductor in a slot. As the
magnets sweep past, its voltage is

16
00:01:07,487 --> 00:01:12,829
flux density, times length, times speed: a
copy of the field's shape, in time.

17
00:01:12,869 --> 00:01:17,588
A conductor further along gives the same
wave, later. For the fundamental,

18
00:01:17,628 --> 00:01:21,511
each voltage is a phasor, lagging by the
conductor's position

19
00:01:21,551 --> 00:01:25,963
in electrical degrees; for the fifth
harmonic, five times that.

20
00:01:26,003 --> 00:01:29,570
A full-pitch coil puts its sides exactly
one pole apart.

21
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:34,336
The return side sees the opposite field
and is connected the opposite way,

22
00:01:34,376 --> 00:01:37,828
so the two phasors add straight: twice one
side.

23
00:01:37,868 --> 00:01:41,618
That's the ideal. A winding factor
compares a real phase with it:

24
00:01:41,658 --> 00:01:45,540
the length of the phasor sum, over the sum
of the lengths.

25
00:01:46,540 --> 00:01:51,435
Now spread each phase belt over q
neighbouring slots. With 24 slots and

26
00:01:51,475 --> 00:01:56,840
4 poles, q is 2, and neighbouring slots
sit 30 electrical degrees apart.

27
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:01,800
Their phasors add head to tail, along an
arc. The sum is the chord,

28
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:06,790
a little shorter than the arc: a
distribution factor of 0.966.

29
00:02:06,830 --> 00:02:13,703
Spread the belt ever more finely, and
chord over arc tends to 3 over π: 0.955.

30
00:02:13,743 --> 00:02:19,369
For the fifth harmonic, the two slots are
150 degrees apart in phase,

31
00:02:19,409 --> 00:02:23,111
and their phasors almost cancel: 0.259.

32
00:02:24,111 --> 00:02:28,471
Next, shorten each coil to span 5 slots
instead of 6.

33
00:02:28,511 --> 00:02:33,521
Its sides are now 150 electrical degrees
apart, not 180.

34
00:02:33,561 --> 00:02:36,754
The two side phasors no longer line up.

35
00:02:36,794 --> 00:02:41,465
The pitch factor is sine of 75 degrees:
0.966 again.

36
00:02:41,505 --> 00:02:46,124
For the fifth harmonic, 150 degrees
becomes 750,

37
00:02:46,164 --> 00:02:51,378
and the factor drops to sine of 15
degrees: 0.259.

38
00:02:51,418 --> 00:02:56,106
The winding factor is the product: 0.933
for the fundamental,

39
00:02:56,146 --> 00:02:59,283
but just 0.067 for the fifth and seventh.

40
00:02:59,323 --> 00:03:04,861
Summing every phasor in the winding table
gives the same numbers.

41
00:03:05,861 --> 00:03:09,664
What about this motor's tooth coils? Here
q is one half,

42
00:03:09,704 --> 00:03:14,594
and the distribution formula breaks: it
gives 1.15, more than one.

43
00:03:14,634 --> 00:03:19,926
So go back to phasors: give each slot its
electrical angle, slot number times

44
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:25,519
pole pairs times 360 degrees over the slot
count. That's the star of slots.

45
00:03:25,559 --> 00:03:30,661
With 12 slots and 4 pole pairs, each slot
is 120 degrees on from the last,

46
00:03:30,701 --> 00:03:34,813
so the star has just three spokes, four
slots on each.

47
00:03:34,853 --> 00:03:38,101
A tooth coil spans one slot, 120 degrees.

48
00:03:38,141 --> 00:03:43,393
Its two sides add to root 3 instead of 2:
a pitch factor of 0.866.

49
00:03:43,433 --> 00:03:50,449
And all four coils of phase a sit on one
spoke, so nothing is lost to distribution.

50
00:03:50,489 --> 00:03:55,699
With 10 poles instead, the same 12 slots
make a star of twelve spokes,

51
00:03:55,739 --> 00:04:00,214
and grouping them into 60-degree sectors
gives 0.933.

52
00:04:01,214 --> 00:04:04,349
Now all four windings, harmonic by
harmonic.

53
00:04:04,389 --> 00:04:07,180
Short pitching cuts the fifth and seventh
together.

54
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,911
That's why five-sixths pitch is so common.

55
00:04:09,951 --> 00:04:14,688
But nothing touches the 11th and 13th:
they keep the fundamental's factor.

56
00:04:14,728 --> 00:04:19,206
They're slot harmonics, one either side of
the 12 slots per pole pair.

57
00:04:19,246 --> 00:04:22,317
Skewing by one slot pitch suppresses them.

58
00:04:22,357 --> 00:04:26,615
And the tooth coils, 9 slots with 6 poles
or 12 with 8,

59
00:04:26,655 --> 00:04:31,844
keep 0.866 at every order shown. They
filter almost nothing.

60
00:04:32,844 --> 00:04:36,053
Now feed in Lesson 5's flat-topped field.

61
00:04:36,093 --> 00:04:41,367
Its fifth harmonic is 13% of the
fundamental; the seventh, 5%.

62
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:46,023
The fifth harmonic has five times as many
poles, each a fifth as wide.

63
00:04:46,063 --> 00:04:52,077
So its flux linkage is the winding factor,
times the field harmonic, divided by five.

64
00:04:52,117 --> 00:04:57,297
But it sweeps past five times as fast, and
the time derivative multiplies by five

65
00:04:57,337 --> 00:05:03,046
again. So the voltage harmonic is just the
winding factor times the field harmonic.

66
00:05:03,086 --> 00:05:07,810
A numerical check, differentiating the
integrated flux linkage, agrees.

67
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:11,928
In this motor, the fifth stays at 13% of
the fundamental;

68
00:05:11,968 --> 00:05:15,901
the short-pitched distributed winding cuts
it below 1%.

69
00:05:15,941 --> 00:05:22,509
And with tooth coils spanning 120 degrees,
the third harmonic cancels in each phase.

70
00:05:23,509 --> 00:05:27,474
The factors work in both directions.
Lesson 2's MMF staircase

71
00:05:27,514 --> 00:05:32,076
has harmonics proportional to the winding
factor over the order.

72
00:05:32,116 --> 00:05:34,516
For the short-pitched distributed winding,

73
00:05:34,556 --> 00:05:38,054
the fifth and seventh are under 2% of the
fundamental.

74
00:05:38,094 --> 00:05:42,963
The tooth coils also make harmonics no
magnet rotor uses for steady torque,

75
00:05:43,003 --> 00:05:48,600
like the second, Lesson 2's order 8: half
the fundamental, travelling backwards.

76
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:52,104
They induce eddy currents in the magnets
and rotor iron:

77
00:05:52,144 --> 00:05:54,897
extra rotor loss, for later lessons.

78
00:05:55,897 --> 00:05:57,770
So why use tooth coils at all?

79
00:05:57,810 --> 00:06:02,557
Each coil wraps a single tooth. The end
turns are short, so less copper and

80
00:06:02,597 --> 00:06:08,126
resistance, and coils wound on separate
teeth pack tightly, for a high slot fill.

81
00:06:08,166 --> 00:06:11,876
Some slot-pole combinations, like 12 slots
with 10 poles,

82
00:06:11,916 --> 00:06:16,553
also give very low cogging torque, though
not this one: Lesson 9.

83
00:06:16,593 --> 00:06:21,668
The price is the 0.866: about 15% more
turns for the same back-EMF

84
00:06:21,708 --> 00:06:27,479
and torque per amp, plus those MMF
harmonics and their rotor losses.

85
00:06:28,479 --> 00:06:33,245
Your turn. Build each winding from the
star of slots: give every coil its

86
00:06:33,285 --> 00:06:39,044
electrical angle, assign it to a phase by
60-degree sector, and fill a slot table.

87
00:06:39,084 --> 00:06:42,792
For each harmonic, sum the slot phasors
weighted by the turns,

88
00:06:42,832 --> 00:06:45,006
and divide by the total turns.

89
00:06:45,046 --> 00:06:53,150
Check: 0.966 and 0.259 full-pitch, 0.933
and 0.067 short-pitched,

90
00:06:53,190 --> 00:07:03,338
and 0.866 everywhere for the tooth coils.
The back-EMF's fifth: 13% and 0.9%.

91
00:07:04,338 --> 00:07:07,098
This picture puts each conductor at a slot
centre,

92
00:07:07,138 --> 00:07:11,587
and treats the rotor field as fixed in
shape, as if the bore were smooth.

93
00:07:11,627 --> 00:07:16,440
Real slot openings modulate the field
itself, adding harmonics tied to the

94
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:21,824
slot count. Lesson 5's solver includes
them; a winding factor alone doesn't.

95
00:07:21,864 --> 00:07:24,141
The end turns, saturation and skew sit

96
00:07:24,181 --> 00:07:27,302
outside this two-dimensional, linear
model.

97
00:07:28,302 --> 00:07:30,582
Three predictions before next time.

98
00:07:30,622 --> 00:07:33,006
One: what's the pitch factor of a five-
sixths

99
00:07:33,046 --> 00:07:35,700
short-pitched coil, for the fundamental?

100
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:39,404
Two: the same coil, for the fifth
harmonic?

101
00:07:40,304 --> 00:07:46,210
Three: same turns, same flux, but a
winding factor of 0.866 instead of 1.

102
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:49,520
What's the ratio of the back-EMFs?

103
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:54,494
Next time: back-EMF. Flux linkage, the
reference motor's voltage,

104
00:07:54,534 --> 00:07:58,198
and measuring it with a drill and an
oscilloscope.
